
This is a distribution of the CDL2 Lab Redux for Windows and Linux. 
Zip files are provided separately for each platform and for both in a single zip file.

CONTENT

CDL2/
  _README                        This file.
  _LICENSE                       The statement of applicable license.
  Docs/
     CDL2 Lab.md                 The Lab User Guide.
     CDL2-vWG.md                 A van Wijngaarden grammar for CDL2.
     hms.pdf                     An introduction to CDL2 by Kees Koster and Maik Stahl.
     Implementation Notes.md     Some random implementation notes.
     md-styles.css               Used by some MarkDown renderers.
  Sample/
     CDL2v1.lab.gz               A Lab DB containing the Quicksort example.
     Quicksort.cdl2              Quicksort example in CDL2 with PowerShell, C#, and C kernel modules.
     LabCommandsTest.labc        A sample file of Lab commands sutiable for the Lab consult command.  
  C/
     CDL2.h                      Standard header file for C code generated by the Lab. May be modified with your own addtions as necessary.
     CDL2Trace.h                 The CDL2 debugger for C. CDL2.h will inludde this file when appropriate settings are used.
  bin/Windows/
    CDL2v1.exe                   The Lab Windows executable. It requires the DLLs listed below to run.
    D3DCompiler_47_cor3.dll
    PenImc_cor3.dll
    PresentationNative_cor3.dll
    vcruntime140_cor3.dll
    wpfgfx_cor3.dll
    cdl2-lab.cmd                 Command to run the Lab on Windows.
    cdl2c.cmd                    Command to run the Lab as a CDL2 compiler with a given set of .cdl2 files. 
                                 It generates a new DB file.
    cc.cmd                       Windows command file to compile the generated C code with debug symbols.
    cco.cmd                      Windows command file to compile the generated C code with optimizations.
  bin/Linux/
    CDL2v1-Linux                 The Lab Linux executable.
    cdl2-lab                     Shell command to run the Lab on Linux.
    cdl2c                        Shell command to run the Lab as a CDL2 compiler with a given set of .cdl2 files.
                                 It generates a new DB file.




NOTES
0. On Linux, execute the following command in the cdl2/bin/Linux directory after unzipping
   to make the shell commands and the lab binary itself executable:
      chmod +x cdl2-lab cdl2c CDL2v1-Linux
   For both platforms the script files will probably require some tuning.
1. Files with the .md extension are Markdown files. You can open them with a text editor or a Markdown viewer.
   Markdown files are mostly readable as text files, but they can also be rendered with proper formatting.
   On Winddows, you can use Visual Studio Code with a Markdown extension, etc.
   On Linux, you can use a Markdown viewer. One option is
      pandoc file.md -o file.html && xdg-open file.html
   assuming you have already done 'sudo apt install pandoc xdg-utils'.
2. The sources are not included in this distribution. They are available on GitHub at
   https://github.com/kovesp/CDL2
   once I make the repo public. The source is licensed under the MIT License, see _LICENSE for details.
3. The executable files for both platforms are built with Visual Studio 2022 on Windows.
4. PowerShell code generator.
   The generated .ps1 file can be run directly with the pwsh command.
   On Linux you must have installed PowerShell already. The simplest way is
      sudo snap install powershell --classic
   Then use pwsh -v to verify that it worked.
5. C# code generator.
   The generated .cs file can be run directly with the dotnet CLI. 
   You need to have the .NET SDK installed on your system. You can download
   it from https://dotnet.microsoft.com/download.
   After installing, you can use the following commands to compile and run the generated C# code: 
      dotnet new console -o MyApp
      mv GeneratedCode.cs MyApp/Program.cs
      cd MyApp
      dotnet run
6. C Code generator.
   The generated .c file can be compiled with a C compiler.

   For Windows use the included cc.cmd or cco.cmd to compile. The first command
   generates an executable suitable for debugging, while the second one generates an optimized executable.
   These commands use the Microsoft Visual C++ compiler (cl.exe) that comes with Visual Studio.
   Make sure you have Visual Studio installed. Note that "debugging" here means both in the sense of the
   CDL2 debugger and in the sense of the Visual Studio debugger (with appropriate settings, the generated program
   will halt right after startup allowing the VS debugger to attach). See the CDL2 Lab.md for more details
   on which settings to use when genrating code. It is of course also possible to use gcc, but I have not (yet)
   tried this.

   For Linux, just use gcc with appropriate flags (works fine with no flags).

   See CDL2 Lab.md for more details on which settings to use when generating code
   to instrument it for the CDL2 debbuger and profiler. The generated code always #includes CDL2.h and
   optionally includes CDL2Trace.h which contains the CDL2 debugger.
